Subject: Planning challenge for a new transmission network with quadratic loss costs

We are planning a transmission build-out connecting four generator sites to ten consumer nodes across a total of fourteen locations. The nodes are laid out on a regional map with kilometer-scale coordinates, and we need to decide which transmission corridors to construct to move power from the generators to the consumers while keeping both construction and operating expenses as low as practical.

In this planning model, two cost drivers matter and they pull in opposite directions:
- Building lines has a straightforward cost that scales with distance: longer spans cost more to construct and to secure right-of-way.
- Operating the network incurs a heating cost due to resistive losses in the conductors. This is not a simple linear penalty; it follows a square law. Pushing twice the power through a line causes roughly four times the resistive losses. Because current is proportional to power divided by voltage, these I-squared-R losses scale with the line resistance and the square of power divided by the operating voltage. Higher voltage is specifically used to reduce current and therefore mitigate these quadratic heating costs; the effect is inversely proportional to the square of the operating voltage.

The engineering constraints are typical for transmission planning:
- Power can only flow over corridors we actually build.
- Each consumer must receive enough power to meet its demand.
- Generators have maximum output limits.
- Each line has a maximum transfer rating that cannot be exceeded.
- Wire resistance depends on the physical length of the route and the conductor thickness. We are using copper conductors, so resistance increases with length and decreases with cross-sectional area.
- For planning purposes, power is mass-conserved at the nodes. We do not subtract physical losses from the nodal balances; instead, we assess the financial penalty associated with those losses as a separate operational cost line item. This ensures we capture the true physics—quadratic losses—without distorting the flow accounting.

The decision we are asking the planning tool to make is which set of lines to build and how to route power across them to meet all demands at the lowest total cost. The total cost is the sum of:
- Construction cost, which scales with the geographic distance between nodes.
- Operational loss cost, which is driven by the resistive loss relationship described above (resistance times the square of power over voltage), multiplied by the appropriate energy cost rate. This square-law treatment is essential; simply summing flows or using a linear approximation would understate the penalty for heavy loading and bias the plan toward overly concentrated corridors. By contrast, spreading power flows across multiple routes, even at additional build expense, may reduce losses significantly and lower overall cost.

Planning data provided:
- Node map coordinates: {NODE_COORDINATES}
- Node classifications (generator or consumer): {NODE_TYPES}
- Consumer power demands: {POWER_DEMANDS}
- Generator output limits: {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}
- Copper conductor resistivity: {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}
- Conductor cross-sectional area: {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}
- Nominal operating voltage: {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}
- Maximum transfer capacity per built line: {LINE_CAPACITY}
- Construction cost per kilometer of line: {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}
- Financial cost rate applied to resistive losses: {LOSS_COST_RATE}

With these inputs, the planning objective is to design a network that meets all loads without violating generator or line limits, while minimizing the combined construction spend and the quadratic, physics-based heating costs. The key trade-off to explore is whether building additional corridors (longer routes and more steel) to reduce per-line loading yields a net savings by sharply cutting resistive losses, given that doubling flow on a single line quadruples its loss cost.
